well i'm pleased to say that john  valentin the british diver who helped  lead that rescue joins us now live john  valentin thank you so much for being  with us  and well done on that rescue just just  talk us through those 18 days  morning it's nice to be here i think  they were a very fraught  period of time  it took a long time to find the children  but once we found them we had no idea at  that time how we were going to get the  boys out and that was perhaps the most  difficult part of the operation most  people in the press seem to think that  once we've found them it would be easy  but really the difficulties were just  starting but eventually we were able to  bring all of the boys  back to the surface  safely as well as the coach  and just reminders for for those um  watching that forgotten how this  happened how you managed to get involved  in this and also why it was that we had  to get divers to get these boys out of  the cave when they managed to obviously  they didn't swim in they walked in  so the boys walked into the cave and  then unfortunately due to heavy rainfall  the cave flooded behind them  and so there was eventually a mile or  1500 meters of completely flooded  passage separating the chamber where  they were sheltering  separating them there from the exit and  so it was necessary  to dive them back out through that mile  of flooded passages and that was why  essentially we needed divers i'm a  member of a cave rescue organization in  the uk and i tend to specialize in in  cave diving and this was one of those  very difficult or unusual situations  that required  our expertise  yeah it was just an incredible moment i  remember it very well and i'm sure that  it it kind of put us in a position of  when we're trying to rescue those  chilean miners as well we're just  waiting and waiting and hoping that you  guys would be able to do it eventually  you did i mean describe what it was like  when you finally managed to get through  to those lads  when we when we found the boys i was a  long way beyond what would normally be  considered acceptable safety margins in  cave diving and so i was very focused on  my survival at that time making sure i  could make my exit  but immediately we found the boys  everything changed and rather than being  worried about myself  i now had to be worried about the boys  and try to be thinking about their  welfare and worrying about how we were  going to work with them to reassure them  to give them some sense that we were  going to be able to get them out  and john i thought on the film depicting  the rescue uh is it any good  i think the film is amazing the  underwater cinematography especially is  absolutely outstanding and  i think ron howard and the whole team  have done a really good job of capturing  what i felt was the most important part  of the rescue the concept of teamwork  the idea that all of these organizations  from different nations all came together  with a single shared goal and we were  able to bring all of the boys out by  working closely with one another  and finally then john just talk to us a  little bit about the work that you're  doing to sort of maintain that idea of  teamwork and making sure that people  stay safe when they do take part in  these kind of activities  i realized that most of my  lessons that i've learned in cave diving  could be transferred both to normal life  and also to the workplace and so i've  been involved with a leadership course  with edapp and  we've put this online and it's available  for free  to to allow people really to get a sense  hopefully of some of the lessons and  some of the  techniques that i've learned over the  years to be able to cope better not just  with cave diving and cave rescue  situations but also situations you find  perhaps in the workplace or also just  that we encounter in everyday life
 

